WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.590
macOS forensics requires understanding system layout, user artifacts, logs, plists, persistence locations, file metadata, and application traces.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:16.589
/Users, /Library, ~/Library, /Applications, /System, and /private/var all help orient the investigation.

00:00:16.589 --> 00:00:29.808
User-specific Library data and application support folders are often especially important because they can reveal preferences, caches, saved state, browser data, and activity traces.

00:00:29.808 --> 00:00:34.732
Strong analysis preserves evidence and correlates multiple sources.

00:00:34.732 --> 00:00:48.470
Unified logs, install logs, application logs, recent items, plists, LaunchAgents, LaunchDaemons, login items, APFS metadata, quarantine attributes, browser artifacts, and endpoint telemetry can all contribute.

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:54.950
Any one artifact may be incomplete, stale, misleading, or missing context.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:59.874
Correlation is what turns clues into defensible findings.

00:00:59.874 --> 00:01:02.725
Collection choices shape confidence.

00:01:02.725 --> 00:01:14.908
Disk images, snapshots, targeted collections, memory capture, log exports, hashes, chain of custody records, and case notes all help support defensible work.

00:01:14.908 --> 00:01:28.645
The analyst should explain what was collected, what was not collected, and how live system changes, encryption, retention, privacy settings, or management controls affect conclusions.

00:01:28.645 --> 00:01:38.754
The goal is defensible understanding of what happened, when it happened, and what systems or users were affected.

00:01:38.754 --> 00:01:52.491
macOS forensics should support incident response, insider investigations, endpoint response, malware triage, and user activity analysis through careful evidence handling, honest limitations, and clear communication.
